# Evidence 36 — the runtime pool's checkout timeout, and where the slow benchmark's time goes (decision log Impl-2 #35)
# Branch impl-2, local Docker Postgres 16 on 127.0.0.1:55432, 2026-11-06.
# Commits: 811dceb fix(pool) · 0370255 test(nfr-val-2) · the docs commit that adds this file.

# ===== 1. Pool starvation: RUNTIME_POOL = { max: 2, connectionTimeoutMillis: 10_000 } (src/server/db.ts) =====
# Why 10 s: outside starvation a checkout waits only for the short transactions queued ahead of it (a job's work
# transaction covers one order, one delivery or one account; trm-fetch calls the TRM source outside any transaction;
# k6's 200 requests/s answer in ms). 10 s never fails a healthy request, and it is far under the 50 s batch bound of
# expiry-materialize and commission-reconcile, so a starved run fails, writes its row and frees its lock long before a
# healthy run would have ended.
# How a starved run is recorded (src/shared-kernel/jobs.ts): the run's end is written in its lock's own transaction
# (JobHold.release(last)), so a run that holds job:<name> needs no further checkout to end `failed` / `Unexpected`.
# A run refused even the lock's connection has written nothing: it logs job.lock.acquireFailed and answers 500.

$ npx vitest run --project solu2-atdd _bmad-output/test-artifacts/atdd/sys_jobs.test.ts --reporter=verbose
 ✓ [S-SYS.jobs.auth] … ✓ [S-SYS.jobs.trigger] … ✓ [S-SYS.jobs.h] … ✓ [S-SYS.jobs.failed] … ✓ [S-SYS.sweep.h] …
 ✓ [S-SYS.jobs.single] … ✓ [S-SYS.jobs.C1] …
 ✓ [S-SYS.jobs.pool] … on a pool of 1 a job is refused at once and its run is failed
 ✓ [S-SYS.jobs.starved] on the runtime pool (max 2, a checkout timeout), a job whose second connection another run holds fails within the timeout as a failed JobRun; one refused its first connection answers 500 and writes nothing
      Tests  9 passed (9)
   Duration  2.36s
# [S-SYS.jobs.starved]: runtimePool(url, { connectionTimeoutMillis: 200 }); another run's PgJobLocks holds job:trm-fetch on one
# connection; POST expiry-materialize → [500, 'Unexpected'], its JobRun ['expiry-materialize', 'failed', 'Unexpected', finished],
# the due order not expired; with both connections held, POST outbox-sweep → [500, 'Unexpected'] and no new row; once the pool
# is free, expiry-materialize → 200 and the unit is released. Bounded by the 200 ms pool timeout, no timer of its own.

